<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="ws24">
  <title>24 Quadratic Forms (LN 2.2, 2.3)</title>

  <page>
      <p>
        <alert>Poll Question</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
         True or false: <m>Q(x,y) = 8x^2 + 2y^2 - 8xy \gt 0</m> for all values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Hint: Plot it in Desmos 3D! And: how does this exercise relate to
        symmetric matrices? </em>
      </p>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs>
    <title>24.1 Expressing Quadratic Forms in Terms of Symmetric Matrices</title>
      <p></p><m></m><p></p>
      <p><term>Definition</term></p>
        <p>
          A <term>quadratic form</term> is a function
          <m>Q : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}</m>, given by </p>
          <p><m>
            Q(\vec{x}) = \vec{x}^T A \vec{x} =
            \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \amp x_2 \amp \cdots \amp x_n \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
              a_{11} \amp a_{12} \amp \cdots \amp a_{1n} \\
              a_{12} \amp a_{22} \amp \cdots \amp a_{2n} \\
              \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \vdots \\
              a_{1n} \amp a_{2n} \amp \cdots \amp a_{nn}
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ \vdots \\ x_n \end{pmatrix}
          </m></p>
       
          <p> Matrix <m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m> and symmetric, and <m>\vec{x}</m> is a variable vector in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>. </p>
          <p> Example: express <m>Q = 3x^2_1 + x^2_2 + 8x^3_3 + 4x_1x_2 - 6x_2x_3</m> in the form <m>Q = x^T Ax</m>. </p>
      </paragraphs>
  </page>
  <page>
    <paragraphs>
    <title>24.2 The Principle Axes Theorem</title>
    <p></p><m></m><p></p>
      <p><term>Theorem</term></p>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is a symmetric matrix then there exists an orthogonal change of variable <m>\vec{x} = P \vec{y}</m> that transforms <m>\vec{x}^T A \vec{x}</m> to <m>\vec{y}^T D \vec{y}</m> with no cross-product terms.
        </p>
          <p><em>Proof in the lecture videos/textbook.</em></p>
          <p><m>\textbf{24.2.1 Example}</m></p>
          <p>Consider: <m>Q = x^2 + y^2 + 2kxy</m> where <m>k \in \mathbb{R}</m>.</p>
          <p>
            <ol marker="a)">
              <li>Make a change of variable that transforms the quadratic form into another quadratic form that has no cross-product terms.</li>
              <li>For what values of <m>k</m> will <m>Q</m> satisfy <m>Q \geq 0</m> for any <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>?</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <p><em>Tip: it may help to plot the surfaces in Desmos 3D to see what the surface looks like as you change the value of <m>k</m>.</em></p>
  </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs>
    <title>24.3 Classifying Quadratic Surfaces</title>
    <p></p><m></m><p></p>
      <p>Our goal: <term>solve optimization problems with quadratic functions.</term></p>
      <p>Take for example the surface: </p>
        <p><m>
          Q = x^2 y^2 = \vec{x}^t
          \begin{pmatrix}
            1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 
          \end{pmatrix}
          \vec{x}
        </m></p>
     
      <p>
        If we set <m>Q</m> to be constant, <m>Q = k^2</m>, we obtain the equation of a circle whose radius is <m>k^2</m>.     </p>
        <p><m>
          k^2 = x^2 +  y^2
        </m></p>
  
      
      
                
                <image xml:id="f24-3-1" width="50%">
                    <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
                    \begin{axis}[
                        axis line style = very thick, smooth, 
                        xmin=-4,ymin=-4,zmin=0,xmax=4,ymax=4,zmax=10,
                        axis lines=center,
                        xticklabels={,,}, yticklabels={,,},zticklabels={,,},
                        xtick=\empty, ytick=\empty, ztick=\empty, 
                        % xlabel = $x$,ylabel=$y$,zlabel=$z$
                        ]
                    \addplot3 [surf,shader=flat,draw=black,draw=none,restrict z to domain=0:9, data cs=polar, domain=0:360, y domain=0:3] (x, y, y^2);
                  \end{axis}    
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            <p>The surface above has a unique minimum at the origin. What about other surfaces?</p>
            <p><term>Definition</term></p>
              <p>A quadratic form <m>Q</m> is
                <ul>
                  <li><p><term>positive definite</term> if <m>Q \gt 0</m> for all <m>\vec{x} \neq \vec{0}.</m></p></li>
                  <li><p><term>negative definite</term> if <m>Q \lt 0</m> for all <m>\vec{x} \neq \vec{0}.</m></p></li>
                  <li><p><term>positive semidefinite</term> if <m>Q \geq 0</m> for all <m>\vec{x}.</m></p></li>
                  <li><p><term>negative semidefinite</term> if <m>Q \leq 0</m> for all <m>\vec{x}.</m></p></li>
                  <li><p><term>indefinite</term> if <m>Q</m> takes on positive and negative values for <m>\vec{x} \neq \vec{0}.</m></p></li>
                </ul>
              </p>
              <p><term>Theorem</term></p>
              <p>If <m>A</m> is a matrix with eigenvalues <m>\lambda_i</m>, then <m>Q = \vec{x}^T A\vec{x}</m> is
                <ul>
                  <li><p><term>positive definite</term> when all eigenvalues are positive</p></li>
                  <li><p><term>negative definite</term> when all eigenvalues are negative</p></li>
                  <li><p><term>indefinite</term> when at least one eigenvalue is negative and at least one eigenvalue is positive</p></li>
                </ul>
              </p>
            </paragraphs>
  </page>
  <page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>24.3.1 Example</title>
      <p></p><m></m><p></p>
        <p>Assume <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3</m>. Construct the matrix of the quadratic form, and classify the quadratic form.</p>
        <md><mrow>Q = 4x^2_3 - 2x_1x_2</mrow></md>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>
